4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,260
[Episode 2]

5
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:20,833
Yeah, hello?

6
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:22,267
I just got here.

7
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:25,600
What floor was it again?
Has forensics arrived?

8
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,400
- You're here?
- Yeah.

9
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:28,367
The bullet went straight through his temple.
Death was instantaneous.

10
00:01:28,367 --> 00:01:30,200
Have you identified the victim?

11
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:31,467
Yeah.

12
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:34,167
But, wow... I've never seen
anything like this.

13
00:01:34,167 --> 00:01:37,648
Wow, this is unbelievable.
A gun murder in South Korea?

14
00:01:37,648 --> 00:01:40,833
So I've been thinking,
could this possibly be...

15
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:42,400
The victim's identity?

16
00:01:44,944 --> 00:01:46,467
Sir, here it is.

17
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:50,700
Hwang Min Ho, 38 years old.

18
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:52,350
The interesting thing is that
he was just released

19
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:53,800
after serving time for murder.

20
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:57,100
He staged his pregnant wife's death
and collected the insurance.

21
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,300
He served three years
and got out three days ago.

22
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:03,200
Just three years?
That's all he got for murder?

23
00:02:04,267 --> 00:02:05,733
It was ruled as a drunk driving case.

24
00:02:05,733 --> 00:02:08,367
His wife, who was
in the passenger seat, died.

25
00:02:08,367 --> 00:02:10,608
They could never prove
it was a planned accident,

26
00:02:10,608 --> 00:02:12,784
so he only got vehicular homicide.

27
00:02:12,784 --> 00:02:15,867
So, I've been thinking, could this be...

28
00:02:15,867 --> 00:02:17,833
Estimated time of death?

29
00:02:19,467 --> 00:02:23,333
According to the coroner, it appears that
at least 12 hours have passed.

30
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:25,067
He had a 4 a.m. wake-up call request,

31
00:02:25,067 --> 00:02:26,833
but he didn't answer, so a staff member
went up and found him.

32
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:29,433
He had a ticket to the Philippines,

33
00:02:29,433 --> 00:02:31,167
so he was probably planning
to catch the first flight out.

34
00:02:31,167 --> 00:02:34,733
Looks like he was taking the money
and running overseas to start a new life.

35
00:02:34,733 --> 00:02:36,267
12 hours?

36
00:02:37,167 --> 00:02:39,400
So that would've been yesterday afternoon?

37
00:02:52,033 --> 00:02:54,800
The window was still open
when the staff member arrived.

38
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:56,933
Clearly a chain-smoker.

39
00:02:57,867 --> 00:03:01,333
Wow... Where on earth
did the bullet come from?

40
00:03:02,167 --> 00:03:04,633
Sir! I found this!

41
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:08,767
It's a bullet!
Wow, a bullet is lodged in here.

42
00:03:08,767 --> 00:03:09,833
A real one-shot, one-kill...

43
00:03:09,833 --> 00:03:11,633
- Keep it down.
- Yes, sir.

44
00:03:55,367 --> 00:03:57,200
No, no way...

45
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:01,200
Even so, that's way too far...

46
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:51,133
What are you doing? What's going on?

47
00:04:51,133 --> 00:04:52,733
I know you're the police,

48
00:04:52,733 --> 00:04:54,700
but barging into
a sacred church this early...

49
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:57,500
Sir, I know this must be a shock.
It was for me too.

50
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:58,867
- Please don't be too alarmed.
- But...

51
00:04:58,867 --> 00:05:03,367
- First, about today's scripture reading...
- You need permission first!

52
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:26,933
No way. Is that even possible from that far?

53
00:05:26,933 --> 00:05:28,267
Can you even see that?

54
00:05:28,267 --> 00:05:31,233
Oh, maybe the gun is just that good?
What kind of gun is it?

55
00:05:31,233 --> 00:05:33,233
Quit yapping.

56
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:36,000
Hey, Beom, what is
the first thing we should do?

57
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,033
Secure the CCTV footage first.

58
00:05:39,033 --> 00:05:40,333
Go check.

59
00:05:40,333 --> 00:05:42,576
If this is the sniper's position,

60
00:05:42,576 --> 00:05:45,367
we should at least
be able to narrow down the time.

61
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,133
- Sorry?
- The time of the shooting.

62
00:05:48,133 --> 00:05:51,900
Not just the church's cameras.
Every CCTV nearby would've been wiped.

63
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:55,967
The moment the footage disappears,
that's the time of the crime.

64
00:05:55,967 --> 00:05:58,400
How could you possibly...

65
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:01,067
Didn't you say you secretly
looked into all this on your own?

66
00:06:04,533 --> 00:06:07,467
Then could this be... As I suspected...

67
00:06:12,733 --> 00:06:14,533
I think that punk is back.

68
00:06:14,533 --> 00:06:16,167
No way...

69
00:06:24,667 --> 00:06:28,200
Nice to see you again, Kingfisher.

70
00:06:29,233 --> 00:06:33,872
- Please take good care of my kid.
- Walk, please.

71
00:06:33,872 --> 00:06:36,700
- I'll see you later.
- Yeah.

72
00:06:37,433 --> 00:06:39,033
- I know a good cafe...
- Oh, it's Yul!

73
00:06:39,033 --> 00:06:41,000
Are you happy I'm taking you today?

74
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,267
Yeah, I like it!

75
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:43,400
Then I should come more often.

76
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:45,776
Yeah, come every single day!

77
00:06:45,776 --> 00:06:47,316
Hello!

78
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,067
- Good morning, Miss.
- Good morning.

79
00:06:51,067 --> 00:06:55,088
Good morning, Miss.
I'm Kwon Tae Seong, Yul's dad.

80
00:06:55,088 --> 00:06:57,300
- It's nice to meet you.
- Oh, hello.

81
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:00,000
- Please take good care of Yul today.
- He says he's Yul's dad.

82
00:07:01,433 --> 00:07:04,700
Have fun today, Yul. See you tonight.

83
00:07:04,700 --> 00:07:06,333
You should do that with me.

84
00:07:06,333 --> 00:07:08,067
Do what?

85
00:07:08,067 --> 00:07:10,633
Oh, this is something Yul's mom
used to do for her every day.

86
00:07:10,633 --> 00:07:12,133
Let me show you.

87
00:07:12,133 --> 00:07:17,600
She goes, "My Yul, today, too,
with friends, get along well!"

88
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:19,408
My Yul, today, too,

89
00:07:19,408 --> 00:07:21,520
- with friends, get along well!
- With friends, get along well!

90
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:22,533
Say goodbye to your dad.

91
00:07:22,533 --> 00:07:25,133
- Bye-bye!
- Let's go inside, Yul. Be careful!

92
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:26,500
Careful. Take it slow.

93
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:28,333
Have a good day.

94
00:07:34,067 --> 00:07:37,767
So she had her husband's help.
That explains it.

95
00:07:37,767 --> 00:07:39,133
He's handsome.

96
00:07:39,133 --> 00:07:41,467
Yul's mom really has it all.

97
00:08:03,667 --> 00:08:07,600
Good morning! You're here again today.

98
00:08:12,433 --> 00:08:13,967
Good morning.

99
00:08:15,733 --> 00:08:18,067
Oh, Bong! Mr. Bong.

100
00:08:20,833 --> 00:08:22,900
Wow, Mr. Han!

101
00:08:23,632 --> 00:08:25,467
Oh, I didn't realize you were here.

102
00:08:25,467 --> 00:08:28,733
What a pleasant surprise
first thing in the morning.

103
00:08:28,733 --> 00:08:31,733
You're so pathetic
first thing in the morning.

104
00:08:31,733 --> 00:08:35,800
What is it this time? Lettuce?
Two bags of it?

105
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:43,667
Oh, by the way, I heard that
Team 2 topped fan sales again.

106
00:08:43,667 --> 00:08:45,033
Congrats!

107
00:08:45,033 --> 00:08:47,333
You don't have to congratulate us
for doing what we always do.

108
00:08:47,333 --> 00:08:49,733
- Yeah, I know.
- I'm tired of hearing it.

109
00:08:49,733 --> 00:08:52,100
We also took first in dehumidifiers
and air purifiers.

110
00:08:54,533 --> 00:08:56,100
What about Team 3?

111
00:08:56,100 --> 00:09:01,600
Us? Same as always. Dead last every month.

112
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:06,967
You're way too soft. That's your problem.

113
00:09:06,967 --> 00:09:09,967
Salespeople have to be
ruthless when it counts.

114
00:09:09,967 --> 00:09:11,467
Exactly.

115
00:09:14,233 --> 00:09:15,400
What are you doing? Get in!

116
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,367
Oh, I gotta go.

117
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:26,100
No wonder your whole team's
got no fight in them.

118
00:09:26,100 --> 00:09:27,633
That's right.

119
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:31,300
Good morning!

120
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:32,833
Come on in.

121
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:36,000
Oh, my, hang on a second.

122
00:09:36,867 --> 00:09:38,333
Good morning.

123
00:09:38,333 --> 00:09:40,067
Keep up the good work.

124
00:09:43,767 --> 00:09:45,400
Director Yoo!

125
00:09:52,067 --> 00:09:54,133
Team Leader Han, have you been well?

126
00:09:54,133 --> 00:09:55,504
Oh, yeah, Director Yoo.

127
00:09:55,504 --> 00:09:57,200
- I heard you're back.
- Yeah.

128
00:09:57,200 --> 00:09:58,567
Enjoy your break?

129
00:09:58,567 --> 00:10:00,633
<i>Break, my foot.</i>

130
00:10:00,633 --> 00:10:02,800
Well, yeah.

131
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:06,100
Others take a year or two off,
but you took a whole three years.

132
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:09,433
Now that you're back,
you'd better make up for lost time.

133
00:10:09,433 --> 00:10:14,200
You're the ace of that team.
Time to make up for what you missed.

134
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:15,867
Absolutely.

135
00:10:15,867 --> 00:10:17,100
I'll do my best.

136
00:10:17,100 --> 00:10:18,600
Good.

137
00:10:19,933 --> 00:10:22,067
Just get it together, seriously.

138
00:10:22,067 --> 00:10:23,120
How do you manage to rank last

139
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:25,933
every month, quarter, year,
and product category?

140
00:10:25,933 --> 00:10:28,200
It's not like
you're some kind of killer group.

141
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:32,933
What's the point of us
busting our asses downstairs

142
00:10:32,933 --> 00:10:36,467
when a team dragging down
the average gets to sit pretty upstairs?

143
00:10:38,267 --> 00:10:39,600
Go on up.

144
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:42,500
- Enjoy your rabbit food.
- Yeah.

145
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:44,033
Go.

146
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:50,233
Shall I schedule a meeting?

147
00:10:54,867 --> 00:10:56,300
[Not Today]

148
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:59,000
- Good morning!
- Good morning.

149
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,533
- Good morning.
- Morning.

150
00:11:03,067 --> 00:11:05,392
Hello.

151
00:11:06,233 --> 00:11:07,267
What?

152
00:11:07,267 --> 00:11:08,333
I heard you got a big one?

153
00:11:08,333 --> 00:11:10,320
- It's nothing new, right?
- Right.

154
00:11:24,667 --> 00:11:31,400
"Musan Car Explosion Victim Identified As
10-time Convicted Drug Kingpin."

155
00:11:32,033 --> 00:11:34,967
Wow, now that's a sexy headline.

156
00:11:34,967 --> 00:11:37,233
Explosion, drugs.

157
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,533
Be honest with me, okay?

158
00:11:40,533 --> 00:11:41,967
You just got lucky with this one, right?

159
00:11:41,967 --> 00:11:43,667
Yeah.

160
00:11:43,667 --> 00:11:46,867
Really? You really just got lucky?
By coincidence?

161
00:11:47,733 --> 00:11:49,733
Bae Doo Hyuk...

162
00:11:50,367 --> 00:11:54,000
He worked with an international drug ring
to manufacture and distribute drugs.

163
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,333
A former gangster with ten prior convictions
for murder solicitation and violence.

164
00:11:58,333 --> 00:12:01,267
And this guy just happened to,
on the exact day you went to Musan...

165
00:12:01,267 --> 00:12:03,568
His last crime was aggravated assault,

166
00:12:03,568 --> 00:12:06,400
for which he got three years,
and he was released two months ago.

167
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:08,867
The moment he got out, he went down
to Musan and started causing trouble...

168
00:12:08,867 --> 00:12:11,867
And after running wild like that,
on the very day you went to Musan...

169
00:12:11,867 --> 00:12:13,800
- Bang!
- Wow, you scared me.

170
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:16,600
- He's dead.
- What's this?

171
00:12:16,600 --> 00:12:17,800
What on earth is this?

172
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:20,667
Do you really think
this was just an accident?

173
00:12:20,667 --> 00:12:23,467
Or is it more likely someone killed him
and staged it as an accident?

174
00:12:23,467 --> 00:12:27,867
Hey, you went after the drug kingpin
yourself all the way there?

175
00:12:27,867 --> 00:12:31,200
You're insane. You still haven't
come to your senses.

176
00:12:31,200 --> 00:12:33,667
You did the same thing years ago, remember?
You almost got yourself killed.

177
00:12:33,667 --> 00:12:35,733
That... What was it? Kingfisher.

178
00:12:35,733 --> 00:12:39,800
You almost got killed
by Kingfisher's bullet. Ah, really.

179
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:42,640
- <i>Breaking news.</i>
- What is it?

180
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:45,533
- <i>A shooting using a sniper rifle</i>
- What's that?

181
00:12:45,533 --> 00:12:47,700
<i>has occurred in the heart of Seoul.</i>

182
00:12:47,700 --> 00:12:50,400
- <i>Some speculate that Kingfisher,</i>
- Speak of the devil, is that it?

183
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:54,767
<i>the infamous serial gun killer
who disappeared for the past four years,</i>

184
00:12:54,767 --> 00:12:57,000
- <i>may have returned.</i>
- Turn it up.

185
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,433
<i>The incident took place at a hotel
in Jeonjak-dong, Seoul.</i>

186
00:13:00,433 --> 00:13:01,667
[Breaking News: Kingfisher Returns?]
<i>A man in his thirties,</i>

187
00:13:01,667 --> 00:13:02,667
[Shooting Homicide in Seoul]
<i>identified as Mr. Hwang,</i>

188
00:13:02,667 --> 00:13:07,267
<i>was struck and killed by a bullet
fired from outside the building.</i>

189
00:13:07,267 --> 00:13:10,400
<i>For more details, we'll soon connect live to
our reporter on the scene</i>

190
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:14,367
<i>for the latest on the police investigation.</i>

191
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:18,467
<i>The incident took place at a hotel
in Jeonjak-dong, Seoul.</i>

192
00:13:18,467 --> 00:13:21,000
<i>A man in his thirties,
identified as Mr. Hwang,</i>

193
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,833
<i>was struck and killed by a bullet
fired from outside the building.</i>

194
00:13:24,833 --> 00:13:26,900
[Jeonjak-dong Hotel]
<i>Considering the use of a sniper rifle</i>

195
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:28,528
[Kingfisher Sniper Incident]
<i>and the victim's criminal history,</i>

196
00:13:28,528 --> 00:13:30,700
<i>police see this as the first such case</i>

197
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:32,533
<i>since the Haegong-dong
boarding house case four years ago...</i>

198
00:13:32,533 --> 00:13:33,833
Mom, thanks for the meal.

199
00:13:33,833 --> 00:13:36,200
Okay, hurry along.

200
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:37,296
<i>Some speculate that Kingfisher,</i>

201
00:13:37,296 --> 00:13:38,800
- <i>the infamous serial gun killer...</i>
- Sun Il, look at that.

202
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:41,333
<i>They are keeping a close eye
on the situation.</i>

203
00:13:41,333 --> 00:13:43,233
Tae Seong is going to be busy again.

204
00:13:43,233 --> 00:13:46,500
<i>Kingfisher is the nickname
for a professional sniper killer</i>

205
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:48,496
<i>who has killed 93 people to date,</i>

206
00:13:48,496 --> 00:13:50,900
<i>starting with the 2010 Semyung-dong shooting.</i>

207
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:52,464
- <i>Despite committing serious crimes,</i>
- Kwon Tae Seong!

208
00:13:52,464 --> 00:13:53,464
<i>criminals just received light sentences</i>

209
00:13:53,464 --> 00:13:54,464
- <i>and were released.</i>
- Come in here.

210
00:13:54,464 --> 00:13:56,112
<i>The killings are characterized</i>

211
00:13:56,112 --> 00:13:59,033
<i>by targeting criminals
with high recidivism rates.</i>

212
00:13:59,033 --> 00:14:01,712
<i>If this incident is confirmed
to be the work of Kingfisher,</i>

213
00:14:01,712 --> 00:14:04,112
<i>it would mean that the infamous serial killer</i>

214
00:14:04,112 --> 00:14:07,467
<i>who once shook the nation
has returned after four years,</i>

215
00:14:07,467 --> 00:14:11,767
<i>causing another major shock
and public concern.</i>

216
00:14:11,767 --> 00:14:16,100
Why would you hand that
over to someone else? Why?

217
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:19,267
It's not about why. You know
I'm already covering another case.

218
00:14:19,267 --> 00:14:21,033
What? The drug kingpin case?

219
00:14:21,033 --> 00:14:23,400
You think drugs matter right now?

220
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:26,667
When just the name 'Kingfisher'
triples the viewership?

221
00:14:26,667 --> 00:14:28,688
Besides, you're the reporter
covering Kingfisher.

222
00:14:28,688 --> 00:14:30,900
Who else would you give it to?

223
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:34,033
Come on, that was ages ago...

224
00:14:34,033 --> 00:14:35,400
What happened to you?

225
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:38,367
You used to run out of the room
the second you heard the name Kingfisher.

226
00:14:38,367 --> 00:14:42,767
And now, after all these years,
I'm in charge of investigative reporting...

227
00:14:42,767 --> 00:14:45,267
Exactly. Investigative reporting.

228
00:14:45,267 --> 00:14:47,733
So what is the one thing
people are most curious about right now?

229
00:14:47,733 --> 00:14:51,867
Isn't it why Kingfisher disappeared
for four years and came back alive?

230
00:14:51,867 --> 00:14:54,433
So you can dig deep into that!

231
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:57,367
Chief, that Bae Doo Hyuk

232
00:14:57,367 --> 00:15:00,567
was a major figure who controlled
half of Korea's drug supply and distribution.

233
00:15:00,567 --> 00:15:03,833
There's a high chance this wasn't
an accident but a murder.

234
00:15:03,833 --> 00:15:05,900
Then who do you think is behind it?

235
00:15:05,900 --> 00:15:10,667
That's exactly what I want to investigate.
I'll really give it my all this time.

236
00:15:10,667 --> 00:15:14,533
So you keep fighting too, Chief.

237
00:15:15,167 --> 00:15:16,533
I'll get going.

238
00:15:16,533 --> 00:15:17,767
Hey! Tae Seong!

239
00:15:17,767 --> 00:15:19,433
Hey, Kwon Tae Seong!

240
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:24,233
Did he eat something weird?
What's gotten into him?

241
00:15:29,033 --> 00:15:31,400
[Reporter Kwon Tae Seong]

242
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:35,200
How badly did you handle the scene
for the news to leak already?

243
00:15:36,233 --> 00:15:40,267
A shooting happened in South Korea.
You really think we can cover it up?

244
00:15:40,267 --> 00:15:41,967
Are you sure it's Kingfisher?

245
00:15:41,967 --> 00:15:44,867
It could be someone else.
It could be a copycat crime, for instance.

246
00:15:44,867 --> 00:15:45,900
Who would do that?

247
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:48,200
It's not just any gun.
A sniper shot from 500 meters away?

248
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:50,200
I mean, besides that guy...

249
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:53,500
That punk was quiet for four years.

250
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:57,567
Why now, all of a sudden?
And why our district of all places?

251
00:15:57,567 --> 00:15:59,000
No need for a long explanation.

252
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,133
Can you catch him or not?

253
00:16:01,133 --> 00:16:02,667
We'll investigate.

254
00:16:02,667 --> 00:16:04,700
Good. If you're going to catch him,
do it quickly.

255
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:07,300
If it looks impossible,
wrap it up quietly when the time is right.

256
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,667
Don't make a big scene for no reason.

257
00:16:09,667 --> 00:16:13,000
The higher-ups aren't expecting much anyway.

258
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,467
People considered the best of the best
couldn't catch him for 16 years in 93 tries.

259
00:16:16,467 --> 00:16:18,767
What makes us any different?

260
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:25,267
Even you, the ace of Violent Crimes,
couldn't catch him once.

261
00:16:30,133 --> 00:16:34,600
If we just drag our feet, he'll probably
move on somewhere else soon.

262
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:36,233
Just until then...

263
00:16:37,567 --> 00:16:39,233
Hey, I'm talking to you.

264
00:16:39,233 --> 00:16:42,800
Hey, you handle the briefing too.
Just wing it.

265
00:16:48,767 --> 00:16:50,733
[Violent Crimes Team 1]

266
00:16:50,733 --> 00:16:52,800
- You're here.
- Yeah.

267
00:16:58,633 --> 00:17:00,133
What are you doing?

268
00:17:01,533 --> 00:17:05,300
I was curious why he's called Kingfisher,
so I looked it up.

269
00:17:05,300 --> 00:17:08,267
Wow, it just means "kingfisher" in Korean.

270
00:17:08,267 --> 00:17:10,633
I seriously thought it was the kind of bird
that shoots water guns or something.

271
00:17:10,633 --> 00:17:15,200
But apparently, its beak is long
and shaped almost like a sniper rifle.

272
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:16,592
It perches way up high.

273
00:17:16,592 --> 00:17:20,333
The moment it spots prey,
it shoots into the water like a bullet...

274
00:17:20,333 --> 00:17:22,000
Are you out of your mind?

275
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,467
Is this really the time for you
to be wasting time on this nonsense?

276
00:17:27,567 --> 00:17:32,000
I checked the CCTV center.
Just as we thought, all footage was erased.

277
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,967
The witnesses inside the church
were in choir practice at the time,

278
00:17:34,967 --> 00:17:37,200
so they were all gathered in the main hall
and didn't see anything.

279
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:40,933
The singing was so loud
that they didn't hear anything either.

280
00:17:40,933 --> 00:17:42,300
Choir?

281
00:17:42,300 --> 00:17:44,667
What about the people who drove,
the ones with dashcams?

282
00:17:44,667 --> 00:17:46,633
I already asked about that.

283
00:17:46,633 --> 00:17:49,267
There were seven people
who drove to the church that day.

284
00:17:49,267 --> 00:17:51,792
But the above-ground parking lot
was closed off,

285
00:17:51,792 --> 00:17:53,667
so they all parked underground.

286
00:17:53,667 --> 00:17:58,667
But the church says they never
blocked the above-ground parking lot.

287
00:17:58,667 --> 00:18:02,833
Of course, they didn't.
Someone else must have.

288
00:18:02,833 --> 00:18:04,233
Yeah, someone else...

289
00:18:04,233 --> 00:18:07,133
What? What do you mean, someone else?

290
00:18:10,033 --> 00:18:11,533
- Come with me.
- What?

291
00:18:12,233 --> 00:18:16,033
Study all the Kingfisher
case files by tomorrow.

292
00:18:16,033 --> 00:18:17,967
Memorize every single detail.

293
00:18:19,300 --> 00:18:21,200
Got it?

294
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:23,000
Wait, all this in one day?

295
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:24,900
This isn't even cramming for an exam.

296
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:26,000
I'll test you.

297
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:27,567
Yes, sir!

298
00:18:27,567 --> 00:18:29,167
Good luck.

299
00:18:33,367 --> 00:18:36,033
[The Kingfisher Case Files]

300
00:18:37,433 --> 00:18:40,467
When did he even collect all of this?

301
00:18:40,467 --> 00:18:41,552
[About Kingfisher,]
<i>Sniper attacks</i>

302
00:18:41,552 --> 00:18:43,600
[are we not catching him,]
<i>are one of the most difficult crimes</i>

303
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:46,033
[or can we not catch him?]
<i>to investigate and solve.</i>

304
00:18:46,033 --> 00:18:48,976
<i>There are usually no clues
left behind at the crime scene,</i>

305
00:18:48,976 --> 00:18:51,333
<i>such as fingerprints, footprints, or DNA.</i>

306
00:18:51,333 --> 00:18:55,800
<i>It's also extremely difficult to determine
where the shot was fired from.</i>

307
00:18:55,800 --> 00:18:57,833
<i>And even if we take the time to find it,</i>

308
00:18:57,833 --> 00:19:00,967
<i>any traces that may have been
left at the sniper's position</i>

309
00:19:00,967 --> 00:19:02,467
<i>will already be gone.</i>

310
00:19:02,467 --> 00:19:04,800
It's been a long time
since I've seen this on the news.

311
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:07,767
It'll probably be all over the news for days.

312
00:19:07,767 --> 00:19:11,033
By the way, everything's okay, right?

313
00:19:11,933 --> 00:19:14,967
You know what Bo Na's like. Don't worry.

314
00:19:14,967 --> 00:19:19,433
Well, I'm not worried or anything,
but... why was her leg shaking?

315
00:19:21,100 --> 00:19:22,600
What about you, Team Leader?

316
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:27,200
Hey, my leg wasn't shaking.
It just cramped up for a second.

317
00:19:31,367 --> 00:19:35,833
Fine. Honestly, as the team leader,
I was a little concerned.

318
00:19:35,833 --> 00:19:38,400
It's been four years
since Director Yoo had her baby.

319
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:42,967
Even athletes worry that they won't be
the same after taking just a year off.

320
00:19:42,967 --> 00:19:48,900
I wondered if she might be getting tired
or feeling nervous...

321
00:19:48,900 --> 00:19:51,967
What if she made a mistake?

322
00:19:51,967 --> 00:19:53,800
She said it gave her chills.

323
00:19:55,767 --> 00:19:57,433
Chills?

324
00:19:57,433 --> 00:19:58,833
It was creepy too.

325
00:19:58,833 --> 00:20:00,800
The great Yoo Bo Na?

326
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:09,467
Wow, this place really hasn't changed.

327
00:20:09,467 --> 00:20:12,667
How do you even breathe, eat, and sleep here?

328
00:20:12,667 --> 00:20:16,000
When was your last health checkup?
Do you ever even go home?

329
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,667
You're here? That was quite a greeting.

330
00:20:19,667 --> 00:20:23,667
Pulled another all-nighter, huh?
What were you tinkering with all night?

331
00:20:23,667 --> 00:20:28,633
The boss wanted me to make something
flow smoothly, like this and that.

332
00:20:28,633 --> 00:20:33,033
So I made it, but I was looking for
one more source.

333
00:20:33,033 --> 00:20:34,467
Oh, really?

334
00:20:34,467 --> 00:20:37,167
So I played around with it
like this and that.

335
00:20:37,167 --> 00:20:40,133
And turns out it's a flying fly.
The fly just kind of...

336
00:20:48,167 --> 00:20:49,833
You changed your gear a bit?

337
00:20:49,833 --> 00:20:54,333
Oh, you noticed? That's Yoo Bo Na for you.

338
00:20:57,200 --> 00:21:01,967
It's a bit lighter and has less vibration.

339
00:21:01,967 --> 00:21:04,833
The ammunition must have changed too.

340
00:21:04,833 --> 00:21:07,167
It's fine. It only took a few months.

341
00:21:07,167 --> 00:21:09,633
- Do you like it?
- Yeah.

342
00:21:09,633 --> 00:21:11,067
Thanks to you, it was much better.

343
00:21:11,067 --> 00:21:15,200
My kid has grown so much lately.
She's 16 kilograms now.

344
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:17,200
My gift for your comeback.

345
00:21:29,900 --> 00:21:34,833
Yes, Miss! What? What is it?

346
00:21:34,833 --> 00:21:36,367
What?

347
00:21:45,167 --> 00:21:48,633
Wait, you mean what made Director Yoo nervous
was because of her child?

348
00:21:50,067 --> 00:21:53,600
Yul sleeps well, and this morning...

349
00:21:53,600 --> 00:21:57,667
Was Director Yoo in there?
Without even wearing a mask?

350
00:21:57,667 --> 00:22:00,567
Could you let me video call her?
I'll try talking to her.

351
00:22:00,567 --> 00:22:02,733
Yeah, I'll set it up.

352
00:22:04,767 --> 00:22:06,333
Yul!

353
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:11,400
Oh, my Yul! Why don't you want to take a nap?

354
00:22:11,400 --> 00:22:13,700
All your friends are sleeping. Why don't you?

355
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:16,700
A monster appeared in my dream,
and I'm scared.

356
00:22:16,700 --> 00:22:21,733
Oh, a monster appeared in your dream?
Is that what happened?

357
00:22:21,733 --> 00:22:25,833
Should I sing your favorite Baby Shark's
My Dream Is a Superhero?

358
00:22:25,833 --> 00:22:28,967
You know all the monsters run away
when we sing that song, right?

359
00:22:28,967 --> 00:22:30,200
Alright, good!

360
00:22:30,200 --> 00:22:32,267
<i>Okay, I'm starting! Here we go.</i>

361
00:22:32,267 --> 00:22:35,167
<i>♪ I'm going to be a superhero ♪</i>

362
00:22:35,167 --> 00:22:37,867
<i>♪ I will save the whole wide world ♪</i>

363
00:22:37,867 --> 00:22:40,000
<i>♪ Bang, bang, bang ♪</i>

364
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,992
<i>♪ My dream is to be a superhero ♪</i>

365
00:22:42,992 --> 00:22:44,792
How is it?

366
00:22:46,767 --> 00:22:49,867
- That gives me chills. It's creepy too.
- You like it?

367
00:22:49,867 --> 00:22:52,600
So, the great Kingfisher has become a mom?

368
00:22:52,600 --> 00:22:56,336
It's unfamiliar. It's very unfamiliar.

369
00:22:56,336 --> 00:22:59,100
<i>♪ I'm going to be a superhero ♪</i>

370
00:22:59,100 --> 00:23:01,867
<i>♪ I will save the whole wide world ♪</i>

371
00:23:01,867 --> 00:23:04,700
<i>♪ Bang, bang, bang ♪</i>

372
00:23:04,700 --> 00:23:08,300
<i>♪ My dream is to be a superhero ♪</i>

373
00:23:15,633 --> 00:23:18,800
Detective Lee Dong Jin, you're here.

374
00:23:18,800 --> 00:23:22,100
You must be working around the clock
with such a major case

375
00:23:22,100 --> 00:23:23,967
happening in your district.

376
00:23:23,967 --> 00:23:26,167
Please have a seat over here.

377
00:23:26,167 --> 00:23:29,133
Reporter Kwon Tae Seong, it's been a while.

378
00:23:29,133 --> 00:23:31,900
You only come looking for me
when something like this happens.

379
00:23:31,900 --> 00:23:33,600
Sorry.

380
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:36,333
Hey, you're paying today.

381
00:23:36,333 --> 00:23:38,733
Come on, people are watching. Sit down.

382
00:23:38,733 --> 00:23:41,400
I already ordered. The most expensive one.

383
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:47,700
Looks expensive.

384
00:23:48,333 --> 00:23:49,567
- Enjoy your meal.
- Yeah.

385
00:23:49,567 --> 00:23:51,233
Thank you.

386
00:23:53,767 --> 00:23:57,600
Just get to the point.
I have to head back right after I eat.

387
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:00,733
You always act like you're busy.
I'm busy too.

388
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:05,233
You're covering the Kingfisher story, right?
That's why you're here.

389
00:24:05,233 --> 00:24:08,733
The juniors handle that stuff now.
I've moved on.

390
00:24:08,733 --> 00:24:11,733
You saw the news.
It's exactly what it looks like.

391
00:24:11,733 --> 00:24:14,067
Nothing more to tell you.

392
00:24:17,567 --> 00:24:22,567
- No need to get you anything expensive.
- Hey, that's mine. Eat yours.

393
00:24:27,300 --> 00:24:29,100
Is it really the Kingfisher?

394
00:24:30,767 --> 00:24:35,233
Oh, my, why does everyone ask me that
the moment they see me?

395
00:24:36,367 --> 00:24:38,600
If I answer, will you write it?

396
00:24:38,600 --> 00:24:41,067
I told you I wouldn't.

397
00:24:41,767 --> 00:24:43,467
- You won't?
- I won't.

398
00:24:43,467 --> 00:24:45,067
Why not?

399
00:24:45,067 --> 00:24:47,200
You're the Kingfisher expert.

400
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:49,900
Now that I think about it,
four years ago in Haegong-dong too...

401
00:24:49,900 --> 00:24:51,533
Is it Kingfisher?

402
00:25:02,533 --> 00:25:06,200
Well, the method
and victim profiles are similar,

403
00:25:06,200 --> 00:25:08,467
but it's still just speculation.

404
00:25:09,333 --> 00:25:11,733
The weapon is different,

405
00:25:11,733 --> 00:25:14,667
and the fact that he rarely carried out
daytime shootings bothers me.

406
00:25:14,667 --> 00:25:18,367
No, forget the official theory.
What's your gut telling you?

407
00:25:22,967 --> 00:25:26,400
One hundred percent Kingfisher.

408
00:25:30,333 --> 00:25:32,933
I thought he retired four years ago.

409
00:25:32,933 --> 00:25:36,100
Hey, what retirement does a killer have?

410
00:25:37,867 --> 00:25:42,267
What are the differences between
the case four years ago and this one?

411
00:25:42,267 --> 00:25:45,600
You were at both scenes, so you'd know.
Anything, even a small detail.

412
00:25:45,600 --> 00:25:48,167
Like, you found anything
at the shooting site...

413
00:25:49,233 --> 00:25:51,167
You're unbelievable.
Did you come here knowing something?

414
00:25:51,167 --> 00:25:53,400
You found something? What is it?

415
00:25:57,633 --> 00:25:59,133
<i>Baby Shark.</i>

416
00:25:59,133 --> 00:26:00,967
What?

417
00:26:00,967 --> 00:26:02,467
A sticker.

418
00:26:06,167 --> 00:26:08,200
What, a sticker?

419
00:26:08,200 --> 00:26:09,733
Yeah.

420
00:26:11,633 --> 00:26:16,000
I sent it to the NFS just in case,
but they said there were no fingerprints,

421
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:20,133
and that it was something you could
easily buy at most stores or online.

422
00:26:20,133 --> 00:26:23,567
Which means every household in Korea
with kids probably has one.

423
00:26:23,567 --> 00:26:25,900
Dozens of Baby Sharks.

424
00:26:25,900 --> 00:26:29,700
The sharks in our house even have babies.

425
00:26:32,500 --> 00:26:35,300
Looks like Kingfisher
is a Baby Shark fanatic just like my Yul.

426
00:26:35,300 --> 00:26:38,633
Hey, this would blow up the views
if I wrote about it.

427
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:47,867
This is good.

428
00:26:47,867 --> 00:26:50,433
Yul, what did you play with Chae Won today?

429
00:26:50,433 --> 00:26:52,767
[Baby Shark]
We played with this sticker.

430
00:26:52,767 --> 00:26:54,133
It's a Baby Shark sticker!

431
00:26:54,133 --> 00:26:55,933
Should I stick one on you too? This one?

432
00:26:55,933 --> 00:26:57,800
Should I put one on? Where should I put it?

433
00:26:57,800 --> 00:27:01,267
Mommy is the mommy shark.

434
00:27:01,267 --> 00:27:03,467
Mommy shark?

435
00:27:03,467 --> 00:27:06,333
This makes my clothes look even prettier.

436
00:27:06,333 --> 00:27:08,767
So what did you put on today?

437
00:27:08,767 --> 00:27:10,700
- Baby Shark.
- You put on a Baby Shark?

438
00:27:10,700 --> 00:27:13,700
This one is cute too. It looks like you.

439
00:27:13,700 --> 00:27:14,767
Yul!

440
00:27:14,767 --> 00:27:16,933
Daddy!

441
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,133
My little precious!

442
00:27:20,133 --> 00:27:22,267
Come here, my big precious.

443
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:29,667
Honey, why are you home so early?
Aren't you busy?

444
00:27:29,667 --> 00:27:32,200
I am busy. But today,
in the middle of all that,

445
00:27:32,200 --> 00:27:35,800
I had a sudden realization
that I missed my wife so much!

446
00:27:35,800 --> 00:27:37,567
What could I do then?
I had to run right over.

447
00:27:37,567 --> 00:27:42,100
Really? Why did you miss me so much today?

448
00:27:44,367 --> 00:27:45,567
You did something wrong, right?

449
00:27:45,567 --> 00:27:48,833
What would I have done wrong?
It's not like that, really.

450
00:27:48,833 --> 00:27:52,000
I really just wanted to see you,
so I came. Seriously.

451
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:56,533
Then we must be connected by telepathy.
I really missed you today too.

452
00:27:56,533 --> 00:27:57,600
Really?

453
00:27:57,600 --> 00:27:59,100
Yeah.

454
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:11,133
What is it?
Something happened today, didn't it?

455
00:28:11,133 --> 00:28:14,767
What do you mean? I'm just tired
from the business trip yesterday.

456
00:28:14,767 --> 00:28:16,833
Since we're already out,
should we grab dinner before going home?

457
00:28:16,833 --> 00:28:18,767
Yeah! Chicken! Chicken!

458
00:28:18,767 --> 00:28:21,167
Yul, do you want chicken?

459
00:28:21,167 --> 00:28:22,733
Should we go to Grandma's and eat chicken?

460
00:28:22,733 --> 00:28:25,400
Grandma's chicken is the best!

461
00:28:25,400 --> 00:28:26,933
Let's go.

462
00:28:26,933 --> 00:28:29,833
Let's go eat chicken.

463
00:28:37,267 --> 00:28:38,333
Enjoy your meal!

464
00:28:38,333 --> 00:28:40,433
- Let me know if you need anything.
- Grandma, Auntie!

465
00:28:40,433 --> 00:28:42,533
Pretty Yul is here.

466
00:28:42,533 --> 00:28:44,067
What brings you here at this hour?

467
00:28:44,067 --> 00:28:46,192
Oh, my, our pretty Yul is here?

468
00:28:46,192 --> 00:28:48,333
My son is here!

469
00:28:48,333 --> 00:28:50,400
Hey, come out here for a sec.

470
00:28:50,400 --> 00:28:52,900
Why is it so hard to see you these days?

471
00:28:52,900 --> 00:28:56,233
Oh, my, my little Yul!

472
00:28:58,733 --> 00:29:01,400
Yul, say hello to Grandma.
Give her a big greeting.

473
00:29:01,400 --> 00:29:03,233
Hello, Grandma.

474
00:29:03,233 --> 00:29:05,467
Good girl!

475
00:29:05,467 --> 00:29:08,867
But what brings you here?
Did you come straight from work?

476
00:29:08,867 --> 00:29:12,000
We ran into each other near home,
and Yul said she wanted chicken.

477
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,067
Oh, did you?

478
00:29:14,067 --> 00:29:16,400
- Then come sit down. Sit down.
- Sit down.

479
00:29:17,033 --> 00:29:20,767
I will fry up
the most delicious chicken for my Yul!

480
00:29:20,767 --> 00:29:22,300
Okay.

481
00:29:23,833 --> 00:29:26,567
Since you're here anyway, stay and eat.

482
00:29:26,567 --> 00:29:30,133
But it's not good for children or adults
to eat takeout food too often.

483
00:29:30,133 --> 00:29:34,400
People who work outside all day
should come home to a warm homemade meal.

484
00:29:34,400 --> 00:29:36,400
It was me who wanted it.

485
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:38,533
Okay, Mother.

486
00:29:40,433 --> 00:29:41,933
I was the one who asked...

487
00:29:43,067 --> 00:29:44,100
Welcome!

488
00:29:44,100 --> 00:29:47,400
- Sun Il, I should've done that. Sorry.
- Oh, please, sit down.

489
00:29:47,400 --> 00:29:49,800
Hey, hold it in today.

490
00:29:49,800 --> 00:29:52,300
Mom's finally in a good mood
after a while. Don't ruin it.

491
00:29:52,300 --> 00:29:55,733
Why? Why is Mom in a good mood?

492
00:29:55,733 --> 00:29:59,133
The landlord said
they'd freeze the rent again.

493
00:29:59,133 --> 00:30:03,467
Other shops raise their rent every year,
but ours hasn't changed for years.

494
00:30:03,467 --> 00:30:06,233
She was bragging about it to everyone.

495
00:30:06,233 --> 00:30:09,267
That's really kind of the landlord.
It's not easy to do that these days.

496
00:30:09,267 --> 00:30:11,300
Such an angel.

497
00:30:11,300 --> 00:30:13,300
We're busy right now, so wait just a little.

498
00:30:13,300 --> 00:30:14,767
Wait just a moment, Yul.

499
00:30:14,767 --> 00:30:16,300
Okay.

500
00:30:17,200 --> 00:30:19,867
I'm back!

501
00:30:19,867 --> 00:30:21,733
Bo Na, you're here?

502
00:30:21,733 --> 00:30:22,800
Auntie!

503
00:30:22,800 --> 00:30:26,233
Oh, my! Our cute Yul is here?

504
00:30:26,233 --> 00:30:28,100
Did you just finish a delivery?

505
00:30:28,100 --> 00:30:30,160
Yeah. Is it some special day?

506
00:30:30,160 --> 00:30:33,000
We got a bunch of
delivery orders all at once.

507
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:34,333
You said you'd drink water, so drink up.

508
00:30:34,333 --> 00:30:37,467
By the way, why are you here
at this hour? Aren't you busy?

509
00:30:37,467 --> 00:30:38,867
I heard Kingfisher showed up.

510
00:30:38,867 --> 00:30:40,784
That was ages ago.
That's not my thing anymore.

511
00:30:40,784 --> 00:30:43,333
What about you? So, how have you been?

512
00:30:43,333 --> 00:30:45,567
Seriously, what have you been
hearing all this time?

513
00:30:45,567 --> 00:30:47,233
Same old, same old.

514
00:30:47,233 --> 00:30:51,200
I mean, I do have a social position
as a graduate student.

515
00:30:51,200 --> 00:30:54,767
But to Mom, that's the same
as being an unemployed bum.

516
00:30:54,767 --> 00:30:56,200
- Ah, seriously...
- That's why

517
00:30:56,200 --> 00:30:57,967
I keep telling you to hire part-timers.

518
00:30:57,967 --> 00:31:00,752
How can we afford
to hire people and pay them?

519
00:31:00,752 --> 00:31:02,533
Family members have to work hard together.

520
00:31:02,533 --> 00:31:07,133
You exploit your daughter for cheap labor.
You're a terrible employer!

521
00:31:07,133 --> 00:31:08,933
A terrible employer.

522
00:31:08,933 --> 00:31:10,733
All you do is talk.

523
00:31:10,733 --> 00:31:12,267
You know your sister-in-law
went back to work, right?

524
00:31:12,267 --> 00:31:15,033
- Yeah.
- If you look after Yul sometimes,

525
00:31:15,033 --> 00:31:20,200
I might, you know, increase your allowance.

526
00:31:20,200 --> 00:31:21,667
Really?

527
00:31:21,667 --> 00:31:26,533
Of course, I'd do anything for our Yul
even without allowance!

528
00:31:26,533 --> 00:31:30,000
But if you offer, I'll gladly accept.

529
00:31:30,900 --> 00:31:34,267
- I'll chip in too. Please help us.
- Done!

530
00:31:34,267 --> 00:31:36,700
Bo Na, how is it being back
at work? Do you like it?

531
00:31:36,700 --> 00:31:39,367
Oh, there's no way it could be good.
Is the work manageable?

532
00:31:39,367 --> 00:31:40,633
[Tae Seong Chicken]
Oh, dear.

533
00:31:40,633 --> 00:31:42,767
Why are you sitting in the customer's seat?
Hurry and go deliver.

534
00:31:42,767 --> 00:31:44,800
How is he a customer?

535
00:31:44,800 --> 00:31:46,333
The deliveries are piling up.

536
00:31:46,333 --> 00:31:47,933
Then at least pay me more.

537
00:31:47,933 --> 00:31:49,467
I'll be back.

538
00:31:53,733 --> 00:31:56,700
Did he go to prison for another crime?

539
00:31:56,700 --> 00:31:59,200
Was he seriously injured or sick?

540
00:31:59,920 --> 00:32:03,633
A vacation... A leave of absence?

541
00:32:03,633 --> 00:32:05,233
You've been saying that since earlier.

542
00:32:05,233 --> 00:32:09,500
What? Is being a murderer a job?
Retirement, leave of absence, vacation?

543
00:32:13,667 --> 00:32:18,533
He could've gotten married,
started a family, and had kids, like me.

544
00:32:18,533 --> 00:32:21,033
Even Kingfisher
could get married, unlike you...

545
00:32:21,733 --> 00:32:23,733
Watch what you say.
There's a gun at the station.

546
00:32:23,733 --> 00:32:25,367
Hey, that's disgusting! Hey!

547
00:32:25,367 --> 00:32:27,184
That's why you should get married, idiot.

548
00:32:27,184 --> 00:32:29,667
Stop living alone like some pathetic loser.

549
00:32:31,280 --> 00:32:35,300
Fine, let's say
your ridiculous theory is true.

550
00:32:35,300 --> 00:32:38,768
If he got married, built a family,
and had a kid, why come back?

551
00:32:38,768 --> 00:32:40,467
Why not just keep living that life?

552
00:32:40,467 --> 00:32:41,900
Because he couldn't stand not killing people?

553
00:32:41,900 --> 00:32:45,100
Or what, because he's some hero
who wants attention?

554
00:32:45,100 --> 00:32:48,333
Or maybe because life got boring?

555
00:32:49,133 --> 00:32:52,333
Why are you asking me?
Ask him yourself when you catch him.

556
00:32:52,333 --> 00:32:55,536
And ask him if he's really
a Baby Shark fanatic too.

557
00:32:55,536 --> 00:32:57,104
- Hey, you.
- Hey, who knows?

558
00:32:57,104 --> 00:33:00,500
That sticker might really have been a clue.

559
00:33:00,500 --> 00:33:02,700
<i>You never know.</i>

560
00:33:16,333 --> 00:33:17,567
Daddy, it's good.

561
00:33:17,567 --> 00:33:19,167
Is it good?

562
00:33:19,167 --> 00:33:21,233
I think it's delicious too.

563
00:33:23,367 --> 00:33:28,100
Yul, you can do this, right?

564
00:33:30,672 --> 00:33:32,233
I'll give it to you.

565
00:33:35,184 --> 00:33:36,600
Is it good?

566
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:38,500
Yul, you learned how to do this, right?

567
00:33:38,500 --> 00:33:41,100
You're a big girl now,
so you can do it, right?

568
00:33:55,300 --> 00:33:57,392
[Yoo Bo Na's Relationship Chart]

569
00:34:07,400 --> 00:34:09,904
[Durumi Electronics Sales Team 3,
Killer Group]

570
00:35:40,433 --> 00:35:43,733
You're already awake? It's still early.

571
00:35:43,733 --> 00:35:45,467
I had a bad dream.

572
00:35:45,467 --> 00:35:50,367
I woke up and got scared
because you weren't there.

573
00:35:50,367 --> 00:35:52,600
A bad dream? What kind of dream?

574
00:35:52,600 --> 00:35:54,400
A dream where someone took you away.

575
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:57,700
Who? Who took me?

576
00:35:57,700 --> 00:35:58,767
A monster.

577
00:35:58,767 --> 00:36:00,233
What? What is that?

578
00:36:00,233 --> 00:36:03,733
It's real. In my dream,
a really scary monster took you away.

579
00:36:03,733 --> 00:36:05,867
You're in big trouble now.

580
00:36:07,433 --> 00:36:12,367
No, maybe I'm the one who's in trouble.
I can't live without you.

581
00:36:12,367 --> 00:36:15,600
Then I guess I should sing
Baby Shark's Superhero for you.

582
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:17,433
Apparently, it's the perfect weapon
against monsters.

583
00:36:17,433 --> 00:36:19,067
- Really?
- Yeah.

584
00:36:19,067 --> 00:36:20,467
Sing it.

585
00:36:20,467 --> 00:36:21,500
Sing it for me.

586
00:36:21,500 --> 00:36:25,867
Go back inside and get some more sleep.
I'll take Yul to daycare today.

587
00:36:33,767 --> 00:36:37,167
Let's just stay like this for one minute.

588
00:36:48,767 --> 00:36:52,200
[Durumi Electronics]

589
00:37:16,300 --> 00:37:18,267
Good morning!

590
00:38:37,167 --> 00:38:38,933
[Security Alert]

591
00:38:41,900 --> 00:38:45,633
Hey, when did Tae Min leave
without saying anything?

592
00:38:47,267 --> 00:38:49,767
[Unregistered Entrant Detected]
What, he brought someone with him?

593
00:38:51,833 --> 00:38:55,433
She's finally here! Our team's newbie!

594
00:38:56,367 --> 00:38:58,267
Wow!

595
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:01,600
[Bong Tae Min]

596
00:39:05,192 --> 00:39:06,192
[Not Today]

597
00:39:06,192 --> 00:39:08,267
What? The case has been...

598
00:39:10,167 --> 00:39:12,133
The case has been closed?

599
00:39:14,233 --> 00:39:15,733
Just a moment.

600
00:39:17,433 --> 00:39:18,967
The investigation is already over?

601
00:39:18,967 --> 00:39:20,700
What about the NFS results?
Did they come out?

602
00:39:20,700 --> 00:39:24,067
They did come out,
but there was nothing significant.

603
00:39:24,067 --> 00:39:25,100
[Bae Doo Hyuk Car Explosion Case]

604
00:39:25,100 --> 00:39:28,867
It has been classified
as an accidental death.

605
00:39:28,867 --> 00:39:30,467
An accidental death?

606
00:39:30,467 --> 00:39:32,767
So you're saying it wasn't murder,
but an accident?

607
00:39:32,767 --> 00:39:37,367
Wow, our new employee
brought this on her first day.

608
00:39:37,367 --> 00:39:39,367
She's got great taste, right?

609
00:39:39,367 --> 00:39:40,467
Yeah.

610
00:39:40,467 --> 00:39:43,500
Then we should do proper introductions.

611
00:39:43,500 --> 00:39:45,967
As I've mentioned before,

612
00:39:45,967 --> 00:39:49,867
this is our new team member
who will be working with us, Ms. Oh Hyun Nam.

613
00:39:51,233 --> 00:39:54,367
Nice to meet you.
I look forward to working with you.

614
00:39:54,367 --> 00:39:56,000
Welcome!

615
00:39:58,933 --> 00:40:00,800
Same here. It's nice to meet you.

616
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:04,784
Oh, Ms. Hyun Nam has been working alone
as a freelancer until now,

617
00:40:04,784 --> 00:40:07,248
so this is her first time working
in a team and company environment.

618
00:40:07,248 --> 00:40:09,300
Please help her out.

619
00:40:09,300 --> 00:40:11,833
Let me properly introduce everyone
on our team.

620
00:40:11,833 --> 00:40:14,700
This is Assistant Manager Yang Ye Sol.

621
00:40:14,700 --> 00:40:18,367
Her job is forging all kinds of documents.

622
00:40:18,367 --> 00:40:20,600
- How well?
- Perfectly.

623
00:40:20,600 --> 00:40:22,064
[Business Trip Application]
<i>Please come to me</i>

624
00:40:22,064 --> 00:40:23,233
<i>for anything related to paperwork.</i>

625
00:40:23,233 --> 00:40:26,567
[Destination: Musan, Reason: Client Meeting]

626
00:40:27,633 --> 00:40:29,200
Alright.

627
00:40:29,200 --> 00:40:30,533
You startled me.

628
00:40:30,533 --> 00:40:35,233
Were you there the whole time?
That's Manager Bong Tae Min.

629
00:40:35,233 --> 00:40:36,688
We don't call him Manager Bong, though.

630
00:40:36,688 --> 00:40:39,367
We call him Manager Tae or Manager Tae Min.

631
00:40:39,367 --> 00:40:43,333
He handles the cutting-edge equipment there.

632
00:40:44,767 --> 00:40:47,167
Well, that guy...

633
00:40:47,167 --> 00:40:49,167
Is he kept isolated separately?

634
00:40:49,167 --> 00:40:52,333
Isolated? Well, yeah.
It's more of self-isolation.

635
00:40:52,333 --> 00:40:55,367
None of us likes going into that room.

636
00:40:55,367 --> 00:40:57,200
You'll understand later.

637
00:41:00,767 --> 00:41:05,033
It's simple. No need for a long explanation.

638
00:41:05,033 --> 00:41:07,433
<i>A portable LPG gas canister,
burned completely black,</i>

639
00:41:07,433 --> 00:41:10,576
was found in the trunk of Bae Doo Hyuk's car.

640
00:41:10,576 --> 00:41:13,936
It turns out the valve was slightly open.

641
00:41:13,936 --> 00:41:15,733
This is...

642
00:41:15,733 --> 00:41:19,267
better known as Dr. Go.

643
00:41:19,267 --> 00:41:24,267
His job is making any death
look like a medical accident.

644
00:41:24,267 --> 00:41:25,367
How well?

645
00:41:25,367 --> 00:41:27,333
Perfectly.

646
00:41:27,333 --> 00:41:29,367
<i>I'm a real doctor.</i>

647
00:41:36,767 --> 00:41:39,200
The driver is going to the restroom.

648
00:41:39,200 --> 00:41:40,233
<i>Assistant Manager Ki Yeong Do.</i>

649
00:41:40,233 --> 00:41:44,267
Oh, he can do anything with computers.
He's our team's genius hacker.

650
00:41:45,200 --> 00:41:47,133
Nice to meet you.

651
00:41:47,133 --> 00:41:48,633
Yeah.

652
00:41:49,867 --> 00:41:53,367
Oh, I forgot to introduce myself.

653
00:41:53,367 --> 00:41:57,008
I'm Kim Bong Pal,

654
00:41:57,008 --> 00:42:00,833
the leader of Sales Team 3.

655
00:42:00,833 --> 00:42:05,367
My last name is actually Kim,
but everyone just calls me Team Leader Bong.

656
00:42:08,533 --> 00:42:10,233
What I do is...

657
00:42:15,500 --> 00:42:18,167
<i>You're saying there are
absolutely no signs of foul play?</i>

658
00:42:18,167 --> 00:42:21,867
<i>If someone put the gas tank in there
or deliberately opened the valve...</i>

659
00:42:21,867 --> 00:42:25,033
<i>Then there should've been footage of it.</i>

660
00:42:25,033 --> 00:42:27,900
<i>But we checked all the CCTV outside the club</i>

661
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:30,667
<i>and all the dashcam footage
from the parking lot, and there's nothing.</i>

662
00:42:30,667 --> 00:42:33,333
<i>It was pure luck. A miracle.</i>

663
00:42:33,333 --> 00:42:36,800
<i>Well, there's usually a separate driver.</i>

664
00:42:36,800 --> 00:42:39,333
<i>But that day the driver
had a sudden stomach problem.</i>

665
00:42:39,333 --> 00:42:42,833
<i>Well, as for the gas tank,
Bae Doo Hyuk must've put it there himself.</i>

666
00:42:42,833 --> 00:42:45,100
<i>You know where Bae Doo Hyuk was going?</i>

667
00:42:45,100 --> 00:42:47,467
<i>His girlfriend was waiting
for him at a nearby villa.</i>

668
00:42:47,467 --> 00:42:50,467
<i>Maybe they were planning to have
a barbecue together or something.</i>

669
00:42:50,467 --> 00:42:53,233
<i>At least it happened
in a quiet suburban area.</i>

670
00:42:53,233 --> 00:42:55,267
<i>If it had happened downtown...</i>

671
00:42:55,267 --> 00:42:57,100
<i>Oh, dear.</i>

672
00:42:57,100 --> 00:42:59,900
Quiet location. Good.

673
00:42:59,900 --> 00:43:04,567
Once the taxi is more than
100 meters away, we'll go.

674
00:43:06,467 --> 00:43:09,700
Now, client meeting starts.

675
00:43:21,633 --> 00:43:23,433
Meeting clear.

676
00:43:24,967 --> 00:43:29,000
Rest in peace. I can't tell you to be reborn.

677
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:32,867
Whether they die or survive,
I make it all look like an accident.

678
00:43:32,867 --> 00:43:34,900
How well? Perfectly.

679
00:43:34,900 --> 00:43:39,800
Simply put, you could say
he died an unfortunate death.

680
00:43:39,800 --> 00:43:41,633
<i>What a fate...</i>

681
00:43:42,300 --> 00:43:44,267
Isn't something a little strange?

682
00:43:44,267 --> 00:43:47,933
Everything lines up perfectly.
Like someone planned the whole thing.

683
00:43:47,933 --> 00:43:50,633
Bae Doo Hyuk wasn't just involved in drugs.

684
00:43:50,633 --> 00:43:55,000
He was known for framing his underlings
for murders he ordered.

685
00:43:55,000 --> 00:43:56,667
<i>It's a known fact in that world.</i>

686
00:43:56,667 --> 00:44:00,133
Who knows how many
other crimes he got away with?

687
00:44:00,133 --> 00:44:02,100
Exactly my point.
Which is why it is even more...

688
00:44:02,100 --> 00:44:03,267
<i>He was such a piece of work.</i>

689
00:44:03,267 --> 00:44:07,433
Honestly, a guy like him getting gunned down
by the Kingfisher is just unsurprising.

690
00:44:07,433 --> 00:44:09,600
Well, detectives speak with evidence,

691
00:44:09,600 --> 00:44:11,867
and you, the reporter,
should write with facts.

692
00:44:11,867 --> 00:44:15,567
<i>With everyone already worked up
over Kingfisher, who would even care?</i>

693
00:44:15,567 --> 00:44:19,033
Anyway, we've closed the case,
so just know that.

694
00:44:19,033 --> 00:44:21,328
And don't call me again.

695
00:44:21,328 --> 00:44:22,533
Hello...

696
00:44:22,533 --> 00:44:25,100
<i>But public sentiment is different.</i>

697
00:44:25,100 --> 00:44:28,367
<i>Because he only targets the worst criminals,</i>

698
00:44:28,367 --> 00:44:32,033
<i>some people are becoming
obsessed with Kingfisher.</i>

699
00:44:32,033 --> 00:44:34,833
<i>The Kingfisher fan club
already has over a million members.</i>

700
00:44:34,833 --> 00:44:36,267
<i>He targets the worst of the worst,</i>

701
00:44:36,267 --> 00:44:40,033
<i>especially those
who escaped proper punishment,</i>

702
00:44:40,033 --> 00:44:42,767
<i>so isn't it natural that people frustrated
with the flawed justice system</i>

703
00:44:42,767 --> 00:44:44,567
<i>are drawn to him?</i>

704
00:44:44,567 --> 00:44:48,967
<i>A powerful hero who punishes evil
in place of a powerless law.</i>

705
00:44:48,967 --> 00:44:52,033
Lastly, our ace.

706
00:44:52,033 --> 00:44:56,233
The face of Sales Team 3, Director Yoo Bo Na.

707
00:44:58,267 --> 00:44:59,700
As for Director Yoo...

708
00:44:59,700 --> 00:45:02,433
I know who she is.

709
00:45:12,800 --> 00:45:16,467
It's an honor to meet you, Kingfisher.

710
00:45:23,000 --> 00:45:26,633
The pleasure's mine, Ms. Oh Hyun Nam.

711
00:45:29,400 --> 00:45:32,900
Let's work well together from now on.

712
00:45:32,900 --> 00:45:34,400
"Together"?

713
00:45:38,367 --> 00:45:40,500
Yeah, let's work well together.

714
00:45:47,700 --> 00:45:48,800
Where's my desk?

715
00:45:48,800 --> 00:45:51,967
Oh, right here. Next to Assistant Manager Ki.

716
00:45:53,033 --> 00:45:56,433
All right, everyone. Back to work.

717
00:45:58,767 --> 00:46:00,467
Oh, right.

718
00:46:00,467 --> 00:46:02,700
I forgot to tell you what I do.

719
00:46:02,700 --> 00:46:04,300
Oh, you're right.

720
00:46:05,033 --> 00:46:06,700
I handle poisonings.

721
00:46:06,700 --> 00:46:10,288
Slip poison into food or drinks,
and no one ever knows perfectly.

722
00:46:14,133 --> 00:46:17,867
So, how about pork belly with water dropwort
for tonight's team dinner?

723
00:46:17,867 --> 00:46:18,967
Water dropwort is great for detoxing.

724
00:46:18,967 --> 00:46:20,167
Then make the arrangements.

725
00:46:20,167 --> 00:46:23,200
- Yeah, I'll book the place.
- Good.

726
00:46:35,600 --> 00:46:38,933
I've finished the article, so it's fine.

727
00:46:38,933 --> 00:46:44,133
I'll take care of Yul,
so don't worry and enjoy yourself.

728
00:46:44,133 --> 00:46:49,900
It's your welcome-back dinner too.
The guest of honor can't miss it.

729
00:46:49,900 --> 00:46:52,167
Then I'm counting on you tonight.

730
00:46:52,167 --> 00:46:54,867
When you get home,
wash Yul's hands and feet first, okay?

731
00:46:54,867 --> 00:46:58,167
And there's curry
and beef radish soup in the fridge...

732
00:46:58,167 --> 00:47:00,700
I can handle it.

733
00:47:01,900 --> 00:47:06,333
Okay, lecture over! I'll call you
when we're wrapping up.

734
00:47:06,333 --> 00:47:07,833
See you later.

735
00:47:13,680 --> 00:47:16,080
[Musan Drug Kingpin's
Car Explosion Ruled an Accident]

736
00:47:25,168 --> 00:47:26,168
[Musan Drug Kingpin's
Car Explosion Ruled an Accident]

737
00:47:28,967 --> 00:47:30,600
That's over, I see.

738
00:47:33,467 --> 00:47:34,800
Yeah, well, for now.

739
00:47:34,800 --> 00:47:37,867
"For now"? Does that mean
there's a second step?

740
00:47:37,867 --> 00:47:40,800
Forget it. From now on,
you're going after Kingfisher, right?

741
00:47:40,800 --> 00:47:42,867
"What did he do for 4 years,
and why did he come back without dying?"

742
00:47:42,867 --> 00:47:43,933
Well, something more in-depth.

743
00:47:43,933 --> 00:47:45,033
I'll get going.

744
00:47:45,033 --> 00:47:47,167
Oh, leaving already?

745
00:47:47,167 --> 00:47:50,267
Where to first? The NFS?
The crime scene? The police station?

746
00:47:50,267 --> 00:47:52,267
No. I'm picking up my daughter.

747
00:47:52,267 --> 00:47:54,433
- Your daughter?
- My wife is running late today.

748
00:47:54,433 --> 00:47:55,967
Oh, boy.

749
00:47:55,967 --> 00:47:57,767
I'll get going then.

750
00:47:57,767 --> 00:48:00,333
Hey, Tae Seong, you punk!

751
00:48:01,433 --> 00:48:02,960
Well, what's gotten into him?

752
00:48:02,960 --> 00:48:05,584
One bottle of soju, please!

753
00:48:07,067 --> 00:48:08,233
Dig in, everyone.

754
00:48:08,233 --> 00:48:10,033
Yeah.

755
00:48:10,033 --> 00:48:15,267
Director Yoo, don't you ever
get to eat meat at home or...

756
00:48:17,200 --> 00:48:22,467
We're more of a chicken family.
This pork is really amazing.

757
00:48:22,467 --> 00:48:25,900
The best part about coming back to work
is definitely the company dinners.

758
00:48:25,900 --> 00:48:29,480
Absolutely. Team dinners
are the best part of office life.

759
00:48:29,480 --> 00:48:30,480
That's true.

760
00:48:30,480 --> 00:48:32,000
- Alright.
- Cheers!

761
00:48:32,000 --> 00:48:34,033
Sales Team 3, fighting!

762
00:48:34,033 --> 00:48:37,008
- Fighting!
- Cheers!

763
00:48:42,300 --> 00:48:43,833
This is so good.

764
00:48:43,833 --> 00:48:44,867
Good, huh?

765
00:48:44,867 --> 00:48:46,667
Want a drink, Ms. Yoo?

766
00:48:49,067 --> 00:48:51,033
Oh, no!

767
00:48:51,033 --> 00:48:53,600
- Don't.
- I'll just smell it.

768
00:48:55,633 --> 00:48:58,533
Director Yoo hasn't touched alcohol
since she got married.

769
00:48:58,533 --> 00:49:04,700
Honestly, that's for the best.
She gets pretty wild when she drinks.

770
00:49:06,267 --> 00:49:09,133
By the way, I was really surprised.

771
00:49:09,133 --> 00:49:11,900
I heard that Hyun Nam
is the developer of that famous 'Mist'?

772
00:49:11,900 --> 00:49:13,900
Exactly.

773
00:49:13,900 --> 00:49:17,000
Now that Director Yoo is back

774
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:18,467
and someone as talented
as Hyun Nam has joined us,

775
00:49:18,467 --> 00:49:22,933
Sales Team 3 is basically
the Avengers now, right?

776
00:49:22,933 --> 00:49:24,267
Absolutely.

777
00:49:24,267 --> 00:49:27,033
By the way, what exactly is 'Mist'?

778
00:49:27,033 --> 00:49:30,400
I guess you don't know about it,
Assistant Manager Yang.

779
00:49:33,833 --> 00:49:35,800
Gather around.

780
00:49:37,808 --> 00:49:44,667
Mist is the name of the poison
that's claimed the most lives

781
00:49:44,667 --> 00:49:46,733
in recent years.

782
00:49:46,733 --> 00:49:49,133
It's about 10,000 times
more toxic than arsenic

783
00:49:49,133 --> 00:49:52,567
and roughly 100 times
more potent than tetrodotoxin.

784
00:49:52,567 --> 00:49:57,533
No autopsy will ever detect a trace of it.

785
00:49:57,533 --> 00:50:02,533
By then, it naturally breaks down
and leaves almost no trace in the body.

786
00:50:02,533 --> 00:50:05,567
What do you think? Perfectly, right?

787
00:50:06,900 --> 00:50:11,433
For our line of work,
it's the perfect poison.

788
00:50:12,233 --> 00:50:14,400
That's incredible!

789
00:50:14,400 --> 00:50:17,033
How did you create something like that?

790
00:50:17,033 --> 00:50:19,433
Well, it just happened.

791
00:50:19,433 --> 00:50:21,300
"Well, it just happened."

792
00:50:22,833 --> 00:50:24,200
But I'm curious.

793
00:50:24,200 --> 00:50:27,133
With skills like yours,
you could've stayed independent.

794
00:50:27,800 --> 00:50:30,667
Why join this team?

795
00:50:32,800 --> 00:50:33,800
Hold on.

796
00:50:33,800 --> 00:50:35,333
Oh, Director Yoo!

797
00:50:37,933 --> 00:50:40,367
I have a debt to repay.

798
00:50:44,167 --> 00:50:45,200
What did you do today, Yul?

799
00:50:45,200 --> 00:50:47,000
<i>Ta-da!</i>

800
00:50:47,000 --> 00:50:49,033
<i>I drew Mommy and Daddy, and Yul.</i>

801
00:50:49,033 --> 00:50:51,267
Wow, that's a great drawing, Yul!

802
00:50:51,267 --> 00:50:53,633
But why did you draw Daddy better?

803
00:50:53,633 --> 00:50:59,233
<i>Because Yul loves Daddy more, right?</i>

804
00:50:59,233 --> 00:51:03,376
No, Yul loves Mommy more, right?
You love Mommy more, don't you?

805
00:51:03,376 --> 00:51:09,392
<i>No. I love both Mommy and Daddy.</i>

806
00:51:10,367 --> 00:51:15,400
I love Yul too. Love you! Heart!

807
00:51:15,400 --> 00:51:16,867
I'll be home soon.

808
00:51:16,867 --> 00:51:19,800
<i>See you later, Mommy!</i>

809
00:51:21,100 --> 00:51:22,300
So cute.

810
00:51:22,300 --> 00:51:24,967
It's a shame no one else
gets to see it, Kingfisher.

811
00:51:24,967 --> 00:51:27,600
You're having all the fun by yourself.

812
00:51:36,133 --> 00:51:39,200
Don't worry. No one would believe it anyway.

813
00:51:39,200 --> 00:51:43,967
Who'd ever imagine
the infamous Kingfisher is a married mom?

814
00:51:57,567 --> 00:52:00,733
Hyun Nam, do you have Mist?

815
00:52:02,800 --> 00:52:08,833
No, not the drinkable kind, the spray kind.
My clothes smell like pork barbecue.

816
00:52:08,833 --> 00:52:10,384
Got any?

817
00:52:10,384 --> 00:52:11,984
Oh, gosh.

818
00:52:18,867 --> 00:52:21,467
A lot of people would be disappointed.

819
00:52:21,467 --> 00:52:23,600
You're practically worshiped as a hero.

820
00:52:23,600 --> 00:52:26,533
If they found out you're living
such an ordinary family life...

821
00:52:26,533 --> 00:52:28,433
You think so?

822
00:52:28,433 --> 00:52:33,100
Have you ever wished you could
go back to your old life?

823
00:52:35,133 --> 00:52:37,867
Even married women say
they think that sometimes.

824
00:52:37,867 --> 00:52:42,800
Like wanting to go back to the carefree,
glamorous days of being single.

825
00:52:43,433 --> 00:52:47,300
Not at all. I like my life now.

826
00:52:48,533 --> 00:52:52,133
Because everything
that matters to me is right here.

827
00:52:53,233 --> 00:52:55,433
I see.

828
00:52:55,433 --> 00:52:57,000
Then why did you come back?

829
00:52:57,000 --> 00:52:59,500
You could've just kept living
that happy little life.

830
00:52:59,500 --> 00:53:01,933
Then why did you join this team, Hyun Nam?

831
00:53:01,933 --> 00:53:04,367
Did you come here to see me?

832
00:53:04,367 --> 00:53:08,067
Well, it just seemed like
you've been watching me all night.

833
00:53:08,067 --> 00:53:12,333
I wondered if you were my fan.
Or maybe an anti-fan?

834
00:53:12,333 --> 00:53:16,533
They say former fans
make the scariest haters.

835
00:53:16,533 --> 00:53:19,800
That's a little childish.

836
00:53:25,867 --> 00:53:30,333
Oh Hyun Nam, everyone makes choices.

837
00:53:30,333 --> 00:53:33,767
Those choices shape a person's life.

838
00:53:33,767 --> 00:53:36,624
Everyone makes
what they believe is the best choice

839
00:53:36,624 --> 00:53:38,933
because their life matters to them.

840
00:53:38,933 --> 00:53:42,800
And I think those choices deserve respect.

841
00:53:42,800 --> 00:53:44,567
I'm not here to listen to lectures...

842
00:53:44,567 --> 00:53:50,733
Just like it was your own choice
to do this work and join this company.

843
00:53:50,733 --> 00:53:55,000
No one forced you in
or pushed you out, right?

844
00:54:00,100 --> 00:54:04,333
If you're looking to play hero,
you've come to the wrong place.

845
00:54:04,333 --> 00:54:07,433
That's for Baby Sharks.

846
00:54:07,433 --> 00:54:09,167
Not us.

847
00:54:11,267 --> 00:54:13,033
What if...

848
00:54:14,000 --> 00:54:17,967
What would you do if you lost
the things that matter most to you?

849
00:54:20,067 --> 00:54:21,567
I don't know.

850
00:54:22,500 --> 00:54:24,533
I've never thought about it.

851
00:54:25,200 --> 00:54:27,133
Because I don't believe
that will ever happen.

852
00:54:43,133 --> 00:54:45,667
[Caller ID Restricted:
I'd like to provide a tip about Kingfisher]

853
00:55:12,867 --> 00:55:14,200
May I take your order?

854
00:55:14,200 --> 00:55:17,167
One iced Americano, please.

855
00:55:22,267 --> 00:55:25,667
<i>Are you going to be late today?
I'm finishing early today.</i>

856
00:55:26,533 --> 00:55:28,000
<i>Really?</i>

857
00:55:28,000 --> 00:55:30,933
<i>I just have one meeting with an informant,
and then I'll be done too.</i>

858
00:55:31,967 --> 00:55:34,600
<i>Then how about we go on a date for a change?</i>

859
00:55:34,600 --> 00:55:38,033
<i>Se Ah said she'll watch Yul.
Guess she could use a little spending money.</i>

860
00:55:39,900 --> 00:55:44,033
<i>Of course, it's okay with me.
As expected, the power of pocket money!</i>

861
00:55:47,767 --> 00:55:49,367
Thanks!

862
00:56:06,400 --> 00:56:07,633
Alright!

863
00:56:07,633 --> 00:56:12,467
Today is your last chance to take it easy,
so head home early.

864
00:56:12,467 --> 00:56:16,733
And you all know vacation days
are off the table for a while, right?

865
00:56:16,733 --> 00:56:20,200
So get some rest while you can.

866
00:56:20,200 --> 00:56:22,133
Good work. Get home safely.

867
00:56:22,133 --> 00:56:23,667
Yes, sir.

868
00:56:26,100 --> 00:56:28,367
But Oh Hyun Nam is something else.

869
00:56:28,367 --> 00:56:31,500
Taking a personal day
on her second day of work.

870
00:56:32,667 --> 00:56:34,900
She must've had something urgent.

871
00:56:34,900 --> 00:56:39,600
Let's let it slide this time.

872
00:56:40,700 --> 00:56:42,433
Yeah.

873
00:56:42,433 --> 00:56:43,967
Go home, everyone.

874
00:57:05,667 --> 00:57:07,024
[Honey, I've arrived. When will you get here?
Where are you, Tae Seong?]

875
00:57:09,933 --> 00:57:11,248
[New Message]

876
00:57:23,216 --> 00:57:26,600
Well, Assistant Manager Ki,
why are you still here?

877
00:57:28,600 --> 00:57:32,233
What have you been working on all day?

878
00:57:32,233 --> 00:57:36,733
I figured out
why Oh Hyun Nam joined our team.

879
00:57:38,000 --> 00:57:39,733
What are you talking about?

880
00:57:40,600 --> 00:57:41,767
Oh, Director Yoo!

881
00:57:41,767 --> 00:57:43,033
Ms. Yoo, you came just in time.

882
00:57:43,033 --> 00:57:44,600
- Oh Hyun Nam...
- Please track where this message came from.

883
00:57:44,600 --> 00:57:46,200
As fast as you can.

884
00:57:49,733 --> 00:57:54,933
What is this? What's going on?
What about Oh Hyun Nam?

885
00:57:54,933 --> 00:57:58,700
<i>See if you can find me. But come alone.</i>

886
00:57:58,700 --> 00:58:00,900
<i>If you're not here before eight,
whatever happens is on you.</i>

887
00:58:00,900 --> 00:58:02,467
It's for Bo Na.

888
00:58:04,033 --> 00:58:06,200
She came to meet her.

889
00:58:12,933 --> 00:58:14,700
What do you mean?

890
00:58:34,933 --> 00:58:38,433
[Archived Portraits]
Oh Hyun Nam. Real name, Oh Seung Ah.

891
00:58:38,433 --> 00:58:40,133
<i>Fifteen years ago, in winter,</i>

892
00:58:40,133 --> 00:58:43,533
<i>Oh Seung Ah's dad, Oh Man Seok,
was released from prison.</i>

893
00:58:43,533 --> 00:58:46,333
<i>He went straight to see his daughter.</i>

894
00:58:46,333 --> 00:58:48,567
<i>That Christmas night,</i>

895
00:58:49,967 --> 00:58:52,833
<i>Bo Na killed Oh Seung Ah's dad.</i>

896
00:58:53,633 --> 00:58:55,900
<i>Right in front of her.</i>

897
00:58:57,900 --> 00:58:59,433
So...

898
00:59:00,533 --> 00:59:03,667
Oh Hyun Nam deliberately joined Sales Team 3

899
00:59:04,433 --> 00:59:08,000
to get close to Director Yoo
and avenge her dad's death?

900
00:59:25,600 --> 00:59:29,833
Sometimes I wondered
if that day would ever come.

901
00:59:29,833 --> 00:59:32,333
That someone might find me.

902
00:59:33,333 --> 00:59:35,700
That someone might come looking for me.

903
00:59:35,700 --> 00:59:37,233
Who?

904
00:59:40,767 --> 00:59:43,067
I hope it doesn't happen.

905
01:00:50,367 --> 01:00:52,233
Where's Kwon Tae Seong?

906
01:00:53,533 --> 01:00:55,933
You ignored the photos,

907
01:00:55,933 --> 01:00:59,333
but the second I touched your husband,
you came running, huh?

908
01:00:59,333 --> 01:01:03,633
Where is he? I won't ask a third time.

909
01:01:08,867 --> 01:01:11,733
This is called Mist S.

910
01:01:13,533 --> 01:01:17,000
Drink 3 milliliters,
and in about five minutes you'll fall asleep.

911
01:01:17,000 --> 01:01:19,867
A sleep you'll never wake up from.

912
01:01:20,533 --> 01:01:23,267
I gave Kwon Tae Seong 6 milliliters.

913
01:01:27,067 --> 01:01:29,333
Have you lost your mind?

914
01:01:29,333 --> 01:01:31,200
Do you have any idea
how much that stuff costs?

915
01:01:31,200 --> 01:01:35,233
What's the price on your head?
Want in on this bill too?

916
01:01:40,167 --> 01:01:45,167
If he doesn't get the antidote
within the next hour, he'll die.

917
01:01:46,067 --> 01:01:49,800
You know exactly what that means, right?

918
01:01:54,533 --> 01:01:56,533
What do you want?

919
01:02:01,567 --> 01:02:03,500
Do you know who I am?

920
01:02:04,567 --> 01:02:06,067
I do.

921
01:02:07,433 --> 01:02:08,933
You do?

922
01:02:10,067 --> 01:02:12,967
I knew you'd figure it out.

923
01:02:14,800 --> 01:02:17,433
Or is that the work of that rude hacker?

924
01:02:19,500 --> 01:02:21,633
I know exactly who you are.

925
01:02:31,300 --> 01:02:33,700
Do you want to kill my husband?

926
01:02:43,933 --> 01:02:45,700
Then kill me too.

927
01:03:40,767 --> 01:03:42,200
Under no circumstances

928
01:03:42,200 --> 01:03:44,467
can we meet with anyone
other than the client.

929
01:03:44,467 --> 01:03:47,767
Because you messed with someone
you never should have.

930
01:03:47,767 --> 01:03:49,567
Things had been quiet for a while.

931
01:03:49,567 --> 01:03:51,067
- They said they saw Kingfisher?
- You're about to see

932
01:03:51,067 --> 01:03:52,800
another side of Kingfisher.

933
01:03:52,800 --> 01:03:54,967
You know the whole sales team workshop
is scheduled, right?

934
01:03:54,967 --> 01:03:58,200
There's a strong possibility their base
of operations is somewhere nearby.

935
01:03:58,200 --> 01:04:00,400
These punks are crossing the line.

936
01:04:00,400 --> 01:04:02,767
It's 300 million won per head!

937
01:04:02,767 --> 01:04:04,433
I think we're really going to die.

938
01:04:04,433 --> 01:04:06,800
Why isn't Mommy answering?

939
01:04:07,633 --> 01:04:09,200
Mom and Dad...

940
01:04:09,200 --> 01:04:11,033
Director Yoo is awake.

941
01:04:11,033 --> 01:04:12,100
Never lose their dignity...

942
01:04:12,100 --> 01:04:14,633
Those darn flies won't stop buzzing.

943
01:04:14,633 --> 01:04:16,700
Maybe I should go deal with them.
